{{# Inline SVG icon for use in post markdown, so authors can drop a Phosphor
    icon into a sentence without writing HTML.

    Use, in a post, with a right trim so the icon hugs the text after it:
      comp.icon({ name: "lightbulb", size: 4, color: "esoliaamber" })

    WHITESPACE CONTRACT: this component must render to exactly the img tag —
    no leading newline, no trailing newline. Two separate failures follow if it
    does not, and both are silent.

      * A leading newline puts the tag at the start of its own line. Markdown
        then sees a complete open tag alone on a line, which is a CommonMark
        HTML block (condition 7). The block runs to the next blank line and
        swallows whatever follows, so bold markup after it renders as literal
        asterisks.

      * A trailing newline does the same thing, one line later.

    Authors used to work around this with trim markers on both sides of the
    call in posts. The right trim is harmless, but the LEFT trim strips
    backwards past the blank line that separates the call from the block above
    it, so the icon got absorbed into the preceding heading, list item or
    figure. That was issue #326.

    So the whitespace is controlled here instead, in one place:
      - the trim marker on this comment's closing tag removes the newline that
        would otherwise follow it;
      - the file deliberately ends WITHOUT a trailing newline.

    Keep both. src/_components is excluded from `deno fmt` in deno.json partly
    for this reason — the formatter would re-add the trailing newline, and also
    re-wrap the tag below onto several lines, which breaks it a third way
    (issue #324). -#}}
<img class="size-{{ size }} fill-{{ color }}-400 inline-block align-[-0.1em] mr-1" src="{{ name |> icon('phosphor', 'duotone') }}" inline>